WEBVTT
Kind: subtitles
Language: en

1
00:00.360 --> 00:04.400
Hi, my name is Emily Yueh and I'm a partner
in the New York office of McKinsey,

2
00:04.400 --> 00:08.450
a global leader within the healthcare
and advanced industry sectors.

3
00:08.770 --> 00:14.200
Over the last ten-plus years, I've loved serving our clients
across a range of super interesting and complex challenges,

4
00:14.200 --> 00:19.940
ranging from strategy to organization
to transformations.

5
00:20.260 --> 00:24.070
I'm thrilled to be here with you today
and delighted to be part of your core faculty.

6
00:24.390 --> 00:28.710
So my name is Mark Matakis. I'm incredibly excited
to join Emily in this course on problem solving.

7
00:29.090 --> 00:34.600
I've been with McKinsey for over ten years.
I spend a lot of my time on capability building,

8
00:34.600 --> 00:38.590
particularly in the leadership and management space.
But problem solving is one of the things I'm really passionate about.

9
00:38.950 --> 00:45.200
I love the way taking a very structured approach enables us
to take a very ambiguous or complex problem

10
00:45.200 --> 00:48.200
and actually drive to an actionable insight.

11
00:48.200 --> 00:54.140
Problem solving is something that Emily and I both really enjoy,
and we're excited in this course to talk about what problem solving is.

12
00:54.340 --> 00:58.600
Throughout this course, you'll be learning from the two of us,
as well as some of our colleagues, alumni and business leaders.

13
00:58.600 --> 01:02.690
Problem solving is something that we're all doing
constantly in our day-to-day work.

14
01:03.050 --> 01:09.800
And hopefully in the course of these next few hours,
you're going to learn a couple of techniques and tools

15
01:09.800 --> 01:13.600
to really take more deliberate choices
about how you go about problem solving.

16
01:13.600 --> 01:17.560
And what's interesting is that problem solving,
when done well, is not only more efficient

17
01:17.800 --> 01:22.110
and more practical — it actually gets you
to a better answer and a better outcome.

18
01:22.570 --> 01:28.450
And so ultimately, I think a good place for us to start
is to ask: what actually is problem solving? Mark?

19
01:29.190 --> 01:32.640
I think the question of what is problem solving
is one that we hear a lot.

20
01:33.570 --> 01:38.900
I like to think of problem solving as the process by which
we make decisions, either for ourselves or with our teams.

21
01:38.900 --> 01:43.200
And ultimately, it's something we do every day.

22
01:43.200 --> 01:48.320
In some cases, it involves a very long-term project.
It could be over many weeks or many months.

23
01:48.650 --> 01:53.830
But in some cases, it's really problem solving in the moment.
You're in a meeting, someone asks a question, and you need to decide:

24
01:54.280 --> 01:57.600
how am I going to figure this out,
and what am I going to do?

25
01:57.600 --> 02:03.000
I think, for me, problem solving is both an art and a science,
and we're going to learn some techniques

26
02:03.000 --> 02:08.190
around how we can take a very structured approach to that —
but a lot of it is in the application and the day-to-day.

27
02:08.390 --> 02:14.000
But there's another question that I hear a lot, which is:
what makes a good problem solver? I'd love to hear your perspective.

28
02:14.300 --> 02:19.200
I think great problem solving, to me,
really is incredibly structured.

29
02:19.200 --> 02:26.690
And structured can mean a bunch of different things. It can mean
that you have a deliberate approach to how you break apart the problem.

30
02:27.240 --> 02:33.200
I think structure can also mean being very thoughtful
about what you're going to tackle and what you're not going to tackle.

31
02:33.200 --> 02:40.390
In other words, being really 80/20: what's the 20%
that's going to drive 80% of the value you're actually going to see?

32
02:41.000 --> 02:44.930
And then the third piece, to me, is really thinking
about how you're going to actually implement it.

33
02:45.540 --> 02:50.400
And that's oftentimes a critical piece of problem solving
that's misstepped or overlooked.

34
02:50.400 --> 02:56.600
So you need to be really thoughtful about: who are the stakeholders?
How are you going to roll this out? What is realistic?

35
02:56.600 --> 03:00.920
What are some of the common pitfalls?
So being very structured, end to end,

36
03:01.000 --> 03:05.280
is one of the core tenets
of being a good problem solver.
